Z||000002||pherder||10-06-2001||06:04 PM||||87/88s had the cast in "Fiero" on the v6 manifold tops.  I have seen reports of earlier models having them also but unknown whether they had been replaced or came that way from the factory as a one off type of deal.<P>Only the 88 V6 Fieros had the internally balanced engines.<P>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||10-06-2001||07:35 PM||||Hey Bam-Bam.....long time no hear!!<P>I might swing through Vegas over Christmas, if it isn't boarded up by then (j/k  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).<P>Has business out there picked up any?  <P>Take it easy. ||||reg||10
Z||000004||Spektrum-87GT||10-06-2001||07:49 PM||||from what i've seen, the 85/86 v6s used a black sticker, and as stated above, 87/88s had engraved. Sometimes i wish it wasnt engraved, so i could get rodney dickmans 'fiero gt' sticker... but then again, no  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> im kinda curious about the difference between the 88 and 85-87 v6's, because i've seen performance reports on an 87 gt and an 88 gt... the 87 was faster, any reason why?||||reg||1
Z||000005||BAM-BAM||10-06-2001||08:34 PM||||  Hey Mike, everything here is fine, how about Colorado? The airport here is back on schedule and running at capacity. I don't fear any boarding up of the windows any time soon, but one never knows anymore. I guess what bothers me is, it seems that there is a lot of opportunists trying to take advantage of this troubled time. I have every bit of confidence in our national defense system and that this matter will be laid to rest in short order. <BR>  The reason I asked these questions was that I just picked up a complete 2.8 V6 today. When I went to pick it up the guy told me that it came out of an 88GT. However, the plenum is the kind with the sticker on it. Are there any other defining characteristics of the 88 V6 that would help me identify this engine? Also the block has 4000 miles on it. That's right 4K, and I got it for free!!!I also got the flywheel and was hoping that there was a way that I could tell weather or not it's neutrally balanced. If you have any  more clues, help a brutha out.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000006||terryk||10-06-2001||08:50 PM||||Correct me if I' wrong, but I think the 87 and 88 had the engraved plenum, but the 88's had a different oil pressure sender (slim one rather than the fat one).<P>So if you have neither, it's likely 85 or 86, if you have the plenum and fat sender, it's an 87 and if you have both it's an 88.<P>Also, the 88's had the alternator cover right?<P>And I'm having a flashback and none of this is correct....<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||maryjane||10-06-2001||09:49 PM||||welcome back Bam Bam! It ain't changed much!||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfiero||10-06-2001||10:35 PM||||I'm glad to har Vegas is geting back to normal.  I had heard that tourism dropped like a rock and that the Strip was eerily empty for a while.<P>As far as your engine goes.....congrats on getting it for free!  You can't beat that price!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>If it has a sticker in the plenum, then I highly doubt it is an '88 motor.  However, that doesn't mean that the PO didn't swap plenums or installed an early motor into his '88.<P>Terry is right about the oil pressure sending unit.....the '88s are mush smaller in diameter than the early ones.  You can also look at the timing chain cover and oil pan.....they are different.  Try looking at the one in your '85 GT and compare it to the 'free' engine.<P>Also, since you say you have the flywheel from that engine, look for a 1"x3" (approx.) chunk of metal welded to the flywheel.  If it has one, then it is an early motor (and not neutrally balanced, like the '88 engine).<P>The stock '88 flywheel is part #10162025, which is cast into the flywheel.  If yours has a different number or has the weight welded on then it is not an '88 engine.<P>Did it also come with the motor mount bracket?  That is a dead giveaway.....the '88 V-6 bracket is "U" shaped and the '85-'87 is "L" shaped.<P>I hope this helps you figure it out.....my suspicion is that it is an '85-'86 engine, but with only 4000 miles who cares?!?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>      ||||reg||1
Z||000009||mrfiero||10-06-2001||10:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B><P>Also, the 88's had the alternator cover right?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>The '88s didn't have a cover on the alternator......they got rid of those in '88 when GM put on those heat shields around the manifolds (unless that is what you meant?).||||reg||1
Z||000010||voyagerspe||10-06-2001||11:12 PM||||hi<BR>ive got an 85, 87 and 88<P>the 85 has the alt. cover w/ sticker on manifold<P>87 has no cover on the alt. engraved manifold<P>the 88 no cover no cooling fan for alt. and engraved manifold and different oil sending unit.<P>||||reg||7
Z||000011||BAM-BAM||10-06-2001||11:36 PM||||  Mike, thanks for all the vital info. With you information and a little deductive reasoning, I have determined that the engine is in fact an 85-86 V6. The flywheel does have the rectangular plate attached and the OSU is the fat style. <BR>  All that taken into account, here is the striate poop on the engine. It was a factory longblock replacement from GM. The guy replaced a blown engine for his girl friend. Well, as many women do, she was very inattentive and ignored the SERVICE ENGINE SOON light. This guy goes to drive it and notices that it runs really rough and is missing quite frequently. After checking several other things, he decides to check the oil. Wouldn't you know it, water in the oil. He suspects that he may have installed a head gasket wrong or something. <BR>  So, here I am, building up my 3.4 eight months late and this offer come along. Me, thinking that it would be great to have the engine near complete before I have to tear down my daily driver, I jump all over it. After I have the necessary parts off that I need, I am going to tear this block down and have it check out. If it's good, I'll bore it out and rebuild it for a friend of mine that has an 87GT. Or perhaps I'll sell it out right. Either way, the price was extremely good. I will have some extra parts if anyone is interested. All the sensors are present and look to be in good shape. I won't need them as mine are all new. Also the exhaust manifolds are here and intact. There are some 2.8 injectors and some other little things. <BR>  Again, thanks for all the info, it helped me make a quick determination. Again the spirit of Pennocks has shown through.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!!  ||||reg||11
